---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PasswordStrengthChecker from '@/components/tools/security/PasswordStrengthChecker.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="password-strength-checker">
  <PasswordStrengthChecker client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to read the result</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Type or paste the password. It stays masked unless you press the eye button, and the
        analysis runs in JavaScript in this tab, so the password is not sent.
      </li>
      <li>
        Compare the two entropy figures. "Naive" is what a typical meter would
        report. "Adjusted" is what the password is actually worth once known patterns are priced in.
        A large gap between them means your password looks strong but is not.
      </li>
      <li>
        Read the segmentation. The coloured chips show how a cracker would break
        the string into pieces. Green segments are the parts that still have to be brute-forced;
        everything else came cheap.
      </li>
      <li>
        Check the crack-time table. The important row depends on how the site stores your password,
        which you cannot know, so plan for the fast-hash row.
      </li>
      <li>
        Act on the advice. The suggestions are generated from the specific patterns
        found, not from a generic checklist.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How the estimate is calculated</h2>
    <p>
      Most password meters count character classes and multiply by length. That formula assumes
      every character was chosen at random, which is exactly the assumption human-chosen passwords
      violate. By that formula <code>P@ssw0rd1</code> scores 59 bits ("strong" on most sites), but in
      reality it breaks in well under a second, because it is one dictionary word plus three
      substitutions that all cracking rulesets apply automatically.
    </p>
    <p>This tool does what a cracker does instead:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Find all matches: breached-password entries in rank order, English dictionary words, names,
        months and teams (each also checked reversed and with leetspeak undone), plus keyboard runs,
        ascending and descending sequences, repeated blocks, years and dates.
      </li>
      <li>
        Price each match in guesses. A breached password costs its rank in the
        list: <code>123456</code> is one guess. A dictionary word costs the size of the dictionary.
        Capitalisation variants multiply the cost by two for the common patterns, or by the number
        of ways to choose the capitalised positions for genuinely scattered case. Leetspeak
        multiplies by two per substituted character type.
      </li>
      <li>
        Cover the password as cheaply as possible. Dynamic programming finds the
        minimum-cost way to explain the whole string left to right, with anything left over priced
        at full brute-force cost over the alphabet the password uses.
      </li>
      <li>
        Add the cost of the arrangement. The attacker also has to try the different
        ways of splitting a password into pieces, so the factorial of the number of structured
        segments is folded in.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Where the crack times come from</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Scenario</th>
          <th>Rate</th>
          <th>What it represents</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Online, rate limited</td>
          <td>100 per hour</td>
          <td>A login form that locks the account or adds delays after repeated failures.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Online, no throttling</td>
          <td>1,000 per second</td>
          <td>Credential stuffing against an API with no lockout, bounded by the network.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Offline, slow hash</td>
          <td>20,000 per second</td>
          <td>The database has leaked and passwords were stored with bcrypt, scrypt or PBKDF2.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Offline, fast hash</td>
          <td>100 billion per second</td>
          <td>The database has leaked and passwords were stored as unsalted MD5 or SHA-1.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Each time is the guess count divided by the rate and halved, because on average an attacker
      finds a password halfway through the search rather than at the end.
    </p>

    <h2>The limits of any strength checker</h2>
    <p>
      Be sceptical of the number, including this one. Three honest caveats:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The dictionary here is small. Around 1,500 entries against a real rig's
        tens of millions, plus mangling rules generated from actual breach data. Anything this tool
        calls a dictionary word is definitely weak; something it calls unpredictable may still be in
        a wordlist it does not carry.
      </li>
      <li>
        It cannot know what is personal to you. Your street name, your child's birthday and your
        first car are easy to guess for someone who knows you or reads your social media, and a
        generic analyser cannot see them.
      </li>
      <li>
        Strength is not the main risk. Most accounts are lost to reuse, phishing
        and malware, none of which care how many bits the password has. A perfect password typed
        into a convincing fake login page is a compromised password.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Read the score as an upper bound: your password is never stronger than the figure shown, and
      often weaker.
    </p>

    <h2>What a good result looks like</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Adjusted entropy</th>
          <th>Rating</th>
          <th>Reasonable use</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>under 28 bits</td><td>Very weak</td><td>Nothing. Replace it today.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>28 to 35 bits</td><td>Weak</td><td>A throwaway forum account you would not mind losing.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>36 to 59 bits</td><td>Fair</td><td>Low-value accounts behind a rate-limited login.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>60 to 79 bits</td><td>Strong</td><td>Ordinary accounts, assuming two-factor is also on.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>80 bits and above</td><td>Excellent</td><td>Email, banking, and a password manager's master password.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Fixing a weak password</h2>
    <p>
      The fix is almost never "add another symbol". If the tool found a dictionary word doing most
      of the work, no additions around it help, because the word is the problem. Generate
      a genuinely random password and store it in a password manager, or use a passphrase of five or
      six randomly chosen words if you need to type it from memory. Then turn on two-factor
      authentication, which protects you even in the cases a strong password does not.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
